For work that must change code, add a feature, fix correctness, or produce a
candidate, choose an implement workstream: {{ reply.kind }} "implement", {{ reply.hypothesis_id }},
{{ reply.title }} (implementation goal), {{ reply.hypothesis }} (the claim to test), {{ reply.task }} (concrete
changes), and {{ reply.pass_criteria }} (observable success). Set {{ reply.continue_hypothesis }} to false
for a new ID and true to continue an existing one; {{ reply.parent_hypothesis_id }} selects its
starting candidate, and {{ reply.evidence }} cites measurements by location and measured revision.
A measurement applies only to the revision it measured.

Every implement workstream must start with a first measurable step: begin its {{ reply.task }} with
the smallest change to the claim that one implementer turn can finish and submit for
trusted evaluation, then say what follows. An architecture-scale hypothesis is split so
that its first step already produces a measurement. A workstream whose implementer turns
end without a candidate settles as failed, and its history row says why: do not assign the
same task again, narrow it to a first step that fits one turn.
{% if not agent_git_writable %}

An implementer's `.git` is read-only: it cannot commit. It submits its working tree for
evaluation, which snapshots the tree including new files. Never end a {{ reply.task }} with a
commit step, and never tell an implementer to commit.
{% endif %}

Fill every free slot: a slot left free idles until a running workstream finishes.

To combine candidates, choose a merge workstream: {{ reply.kind }} "merge", a new {{ reply.merge_id }},
{{ reply.candidates }} (hypothesis IDs whose latest verified candidates to merge into main, in
the order to apply them), {{ reply.instructions }} (how to combine them, and how to resolve
conflicts), and {{ reply.watch_for }} (what the merger must check or be careful of). A new merger
session applies them on a checkout of main and runs the tests; when its commit passes the
trusted accuracy check and measures no worse than main (a headline metric no worse, and no
benchmark check main passed now failing), main moves to it and its measurement is reported here.
A merge may name only candidates listed as mergeable below. A merger that cannot follow the
instructions refuses and main stays where it is. Only one merge runs at a
time{% if merge_running %}, and merge `{{ merge_running }}` is running now: schedule no other
merge until it finishes{% endif %}. A hypothesis that continues after main moved rebases onto
main first.
