To build one new candidate out of several buildable candidates, choose a combine workstream:
{{ reply.kind }} "combine", a new {{ reply.hypothesis_id }}, {{ reply.title }}, {{ reply.hypothesis }} (why the parts work
better together than alone), {{ reply.parents }} (the parts in the order to apply them, each an
exact offered pair of {{ reply.hypothesis_id }} and {{ reply.revision }} from the buildable list, each a different
hypothesis), {{ reply.instructions }} (how to combine them, and which side wins a conflict),
{{ reply.watch_for }} and {{ reply.pass_criteria }}. A new implementer applies each part's own change on a
checkout of main, runs the tests and submits. The composite is a candidate of the new
hypothesis, measured and judged like any other; it does not move main. Its history row shows
its trusted result beside each part's, with a verdict against the best part, and a composite
that beats main can then be merged.
A part need not beat main or the input. Combine when parts were designed to work together and
each lost or tied alone, when several candidates each measured within noise of the bar, or when
a part's benefit depends on another (a design document naming parts that need each other).
Each combine takes a slot: combine parts for such a reason, not every pair.
